From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mac80211: a station on a new BSS replaces its entry on the old
 one

With IEEE80211_HW_NEEDS_UNIQUE_STA_ADDR a station that turns up on a
second BSS of the radio is refused with -ENOTUNIQ for as long as the
first BSS has it. hostapd does tell the first BSS to drop it, but takes
the kernel entry out only when the status of the deauth frame is back,
after it has tried to add the new one - so the first attempt of every
such move fails. And it does not even do that while it still has an
entry of its own for the station on the new BSS, which it keeps for up
to half a minute after the station has left: a station that goes back
within that time is refused until hostapd times the old one out.

The old association is void the moment the station authenticates
elsewhere on the radio. Between two AP interfaces of different BSSes,
remove the old entry and let the new one in; everything else keeps the
refusal. hostapd ignores the DEL_STATION event of a mac80211 AP and
takes -ENOENT from its own removal later.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>

--- a/net/mac80211/sta_info.c
+++ b/net/mac80211/sta_info.c
@@ -849,10 +849,17 @@ struct sta_info *sta_info_alloc_with_lin
 	return __sta_info_alloc(sdata, mld_addr, link_id, link_addr, gfp);
 }
 
+static bool sta_info_ap_iface(struct ieee80211_sub_if_data *sdata)
+{
+	return sdata->vif.type == NL80211_IFTYPE_AP ||
+	       sdata->vif.type == NL80211_IFTYPE_AP_VLAN;
+}
+
 static int sta_info_insert_check(struct sta_info *sta)
 {
 	struct ieee80211_sub_if_data *sdata = sta->sdata;
 	struct ieee80211_sta *same_addr_sta;
+	struct sta_info *old;
 
 	lockdep_assert_wiphy(sdata->local->hw.wiphy);
 
@@ -880,7 +887,21 @@ static int sta_info_insert_check(struct
 						     sta->addr, NULL);
 	/* For NAN, a peer can re-use */
 	if (same_addr_sta && same_addr_sta != rcu_access_pointer(sta->sta.nmi)) {
+		old = container_of(same_addr_sta, struct sta_info, sta);
 		rcu_read_unlock();
+
+		/* A station that turns up on another BSS of the radio has
+		 * left the one it was on, whatever user space still holds
+		 * for it there: hostapd takes the old entry out only after
+		 * it has added the new one, and not at all while it has a
+		 * stale entry of its own for the new BSS. Take the old one
+		 * out here instead of refusing the new one. The wiphy mutex
+		 * keeps it alive past the RCU section.
+		 */
+		if (sta_info_ap_iface(sdata) && sta_info_ap_iface(old->sdata) &&
+		    old->sdata->bss != sdata->bss && !__sta_info_destroy(old))
+			return 0;
+
 		return -ENOTUNIQ;
 	}
 	rcu_read_unlock();
